{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"7a4f197ab563b8a68b7c882706ca9c4475599e0dd489a9cd57cd315d8ca7d5f1","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_31ec1edcfb270403c67ac9b68aec5201a7a2e691d6d3954697f49cb2d01bd4d5","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a technically coherent extension of Bayesian Moment Matching to Hidden Markov Models for online transfer learning. The methodological framework is well-defined, and the experimental evaluation across three distinct domains is comprehensive, utilizing appropriate statistical tests to validate the improvements over baselines. The primary limitations concern the lack of ablation studies to isolate the contribution of the transfer mechanism and the omission of specific hyperparameter values, which slightly hinders full reproducibility.","paper_id":"ByqiJIqxg","paper_identity_sha256":"6a20c847507678c20b943621d4540729db14307fe6890759dcc58eeec94add58","prompt_identity_sha256":"cd283e9af4ec8003f64d3020bc3339fda5467beffdce3eed81e2a9f828d9138e","raw_response_sha256":"4d46932675d5c36eea4e4849339f6c60fd5f2f324c9b6ea39fb90906d8e72feb","request_identity_sha256":"38bfe0558ddcc2b01401be8b7d4bd602f9e0d94b28b16936eafd28be3d4f2256","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"61588d8f9a9911eaefac772278ee145442f8e5ed3853da8363f2cfc003a74c03","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-011","SRC-015","SRC-025","SRC-026"],"review_text":"The proposed method is technically sound and mathematically consistent. The authors correctly identify the intractability of exact Bayesian learning for HMMs with GMM emissions due to the exponential growth of posterior terms. The adaptation of Bayesian Moment Matching (BMM) to this setting is well-formulated, utilizing a tractable family of distributions (Dirichlet and Normal-Wishart) to approximate the posterior by matching sufficient moments. The derivation of the weighted combination of basis models for the target domain (Equation 4) is logically consistent with the stated objective of online transfer learning. No major conceptual or implementation errors are apparent in the core algorithmic description.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-032","SRC-033","SRC-034","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The experimental design is robust, employing three distinct real-world datasets (activity recognition, sleep classification, and flow direction prediction) to demonstrate generalizability. The use of leave-one-out cross-validation is appropriate for the subject variability problem. However, the evaluation lacks ablation studies. The paper compares the full transfer learning algorithm against a homogeneous baseline and EM, but does not isolate the specific contribution of the online basis weight estimation from the underlying HMM-GMM structure. Additionally, while the datasets are described, the specific selection criteria for the 50 basis models in the sleep study (out of 142) are mentioned as random, but the impact of this number on performance is not systematically explored.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-030","SRC-031","SRC-035"],"review_text":"The paper provides a clear algorithmic outline (Algorithm 1) and describes the data preprocessing steps for each domain. However, specific implementation details required for full reproducibility are missing. The paper does not specify the hyperparameters for the prior distributions (e.g., Dirichlet alpha, Normal-Wishart parameters) used in the BMM algorithm. Furthermore, the number of hidden states (N) and the number of GMM components (M) for each specific experiment are not explicitly listed in the main text, although they are mentioned as inputs to the algorithm. The code availability is not stated.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-006","SRC-007","SRC-008","SRC-032"],"review_text":"The paper positions its work well within the context of transfer learning for subject variability, citing relevant prior work on offline techniques (e.g., Chieu et al., Zhao et al.) and boosting-based transfer learning. The comparison against a homogeneous baseline and the EM algorithm is appropriate for demonstrating the benefit of the proposed online transfer mechanism. The related work section adequately distinguishes the proposed online approach from existing offline methods. However, the comparison is limited to these two baselines; comparisons with other recent online sequence modeling or transfer learning baselines are not provided, though the paper justifies the focus on the specific online nature of the contribution.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-033","SRC-035","SRC-036"],"review_text":"The statistical rigor is appropriate for the experimental setup. The authors report results based on 10 experimental runs for each individual to account for variance. They employ the Wilcoxon signed-rank test to determine the statistical significance of the improvements over the baseline, reporting p-values less than 0.05. The use of a non-parametric test is suitable given the paired nature of the comparisons (same target individual across methods). The reporting of significance markers in the tables (up/down arrows) provides a clear indication of where the improvements are statistically meaningful.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"e936509ea0d1e03b1155d1d725fd687033ab6efd7bd4299d6def5a1c78b71749","paper_id":"ByqiJIqxg","paper_ordinal":80,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"4d46932675d5c36eea4e4849339f6c60fd5f2f324c9b6ea39fb90906d8e72feb","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"ByqiJIqxg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"37109a70eab2ef7fba78a971a6858419fe94b6e4638bf01bbc9138a96f46dd96","request_identity_sh